A||||3||GWMORGAN||Bleeding By Gravity.....||||6
Z||000000||GWMORGAN||11-28-1999||09:24 PM||||Recently, my 86 5spd.(Isuzu) became very hard to shift.I bled the hydraulic system (as per Chilton, Haynes manuals and V8 archie's) and still had the same problem, &lt;&lt;S****Y SHIFTING&gt;&gt;. Out of desperation, I parked the car (nose up) on the steepest hill I could find and opened the slave cylinder bleeder valve and let it bled, while constantly topping off the master cylinder. No pedal pushing, no opening and closing bleeder valve. I then went for a test drive, and boy, my fiero had never shifted better.<BR>Has anyone ever done this before? Anyone who is about to bleed their system, give it a try and let us know.<BR>Happy motoring Guys.||||reg||6
Z||000001||RZURCTN||11-28-1999||11:30 PM||||Works great in a nice warm garage with the front of the car jacked up too!  You just have to make sure that your master cylinder is higher than the slave.  A lot of people don't do this becase in most systems it'll either take forever or it won't work at all.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Thomas||11-29-1999||09:56 AM||||I think, that is what People mean with "work around" or "improvement"||||reg||14
Z||000003||theogre||11-29-1999||10:12 PM||||I gravity bleed the front brakes on my caravan by gravity. The back ones run to slow. same problem on most cars. Not enough hight dif to make it run good allot of the time. When it works there's no reason not to do it this way.||||reg||1
